  "query_preview": "..An upper low across central Nevada will approach\nArizona today, then move across far northern parts of the state\nthis evening into Wednesday morning. Winds aloft ahead of the low\ncontinue to be elevated, resulting in southwesterly surface winds\nof 15-25 mph gusting 30-45 mph through the day. While the heaviest\nrainfall with this pattern is over, there is still enough moisture\nfor plenty of showers and an isolated thunderstorm through the\nday, most widespread over the higher terrain upslope regions. \nHeavy rainfall over the past several days continues to cause\nelevated flows on area creeks and rivers, especially the Verde\nRiver system, including West Clear Creek and the East Verde River.\nA Flood Warning is in effect until 8 AM in this area, primarily\nsouth of Camp Verde. As the low moves into Arizona later this \nevening and overnight, snow levels will to 7000 ft or slightly \nlower, with some light accumulation possible over the highest \nportions of the San Francisco Peaks, Kaibab Plateau, and Chuska \nMtns. Showers may linger into Wednesday afternoon in far northeast\nArizona, but most of the the region will dry out on Wednesday. \nWith the much colder air mass moving in tonight, some higher \nelevation areas (Flagstaff/Williams/Tusayan) could see their first\nhard freeze of the season though this will depend on how quickly \nskies clear and winds drop off. Some frost will also be possible \nas low as 5000 feet including the Prescott region.\n\nThursday and Friday - a weak upper low is forecast to cut off from\nSW Arizona to northern Baja, with strong surface high pressure\ndiving southward to our east. This will produce tight\neast/northeast pressure gradients in our region, leading to gusty\nwinds. Sustained speeds of 20-30 mph with gusts 35-45 mph are\ncurrently forecast for much of the region. The strongest winds in\nthis pattern are typically along and just south of the Mogollon\nRim and Yavapai County mountains. Some moisture could also move \nback into the region from the southeast by Friday into the \nweekend, and a low (10-20%) chance of showers and thunderstorms is\nincluded in our latest forecasts. Rain chances may increase as we\nget closer, depending on the track of the weak upper low as it \nejects northeastward over the weekend. Daytime highs look to stay \nbelow normal into the weekend.",
